  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Weak high pressure dominates with a warming \ntrend through most of Friday.\n\nWeak high pressure slowly sliding eastward off the coast will\ndominate through Thursday. This will provide a warming trend\nwith southwesterly flow and no significant weather, though\npassing disturbances on the north side of the high will keep\nsome clouds around at times, so not close to a wall-to-wall\nclear sky at any time. Thickest clouds likely overnight tonight,\ndecreasing in coverage as the day wears on Thursday. The \nsouthwest flow and clouds will moderate temps, with low 60s \ncommon tonight and near 80 for much of the area on Thursday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Tidal flooding continues to recede in the back \nbays of New Jersey and Delaware.\n\nTidal levels continue to decline and recede as offshore flow \ncontinues to allow the back bays to drain. Some of the back \nbays of Barnegat Bay, Rehoboth Bay, Jefferson Creek and Little \nAssawoman Bay may see some spotty minor tidal flooding continue \nthrough Thursday, as winds turn more shore parallel and slow the\nrate of drainage. However, the bulk of the threat is over. As a\nresult, no coastal flooding headlines are currently in effect.\n\nNo further tidal flooding is expected elsewhere.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Unsettled conditions late this week and late \nthis weekend as a cold front passes through the region Friday \nnight, followed by low pressure on Sunday.\n\nA weak cold front will pass through the region late Friday and \nFriday night and will settle over the Mid-Atlantic on Saturday. \nWeak low pressure then develops on that stalled boundary on \nSunday and passes through the Mid-Atlantic and Delmarva Sunday \nthrough Sunday night.\n\nScattered showers will develop Friday afternoon, mainly north \nand west of the I-95 corridor, then scattered showers spread \neast Friday night. Showers taper off Saturday morning over \nDelmarva as the front slides south of Delmarva and becomes \nnearly stationary over the Mid- Atlantic. Overall, QPF will be \nless than 1/10 inch, though up to 2/10 inch possible in Delmarva\nwhere showers will linger a bit longer.\n\nHigh pressure then builds into the region Saturday. Mostly \nsunny skies for most of the area and partly to mostly cloudy \nskies across Delmarva and extreme southern New Jersey with \nseasonable temperatures with highs in the upper 60s to around \n70.\n\nLow pressure then develops on that boundary and passes through \nthe Mid-Atlantic and Delmarva Sunday and Sunday night. Models \nare bouncing a bit back and forth with how far north that will \ntrack and how far north the precipitation will spread. Previous \nmodels kept the low south of the area with minimal \nprecipitation, and the 12Z suite of models are bringing low back\nfarther north. Best chances for rain will be over Delmarva and \nsouthern New Jersey. \n\nHigh pressure then builds into the region for the start of the \nnew work week.",
